---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["split-shift-pay"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A split shift is a working day broken into two or more parts by a long
    unpaid gap the employer sets, like a restaurant shift of 11:00 AM to
    2:00 PM and then 5:00 PM to 10:00 PM. To get your hours,
    <strong>add up the working parts and leave out the gap</strong>. In most
    places that's all there is to it. In <strong>California</strong>,
    lower-paid employees are also owed a split shift premium of up to one
    hour's pay at minimum wage.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculating hours on a split shift</h2>
  <p>Work out each part of the day on its own, then add them up:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Times</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Lunch service</td><td>11:00 AM – 2:00 PM</td><td>3.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dinner service</td><td>5:00 PM – 10:00 PM</td><td>5.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Total worked</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>8.00</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The 3-hour gap is unpaid and doesn't count toward your hours. Any paid
    rest breaks inside the working parts do count. Overtime rules use the
    total for the day or week as usual, so in California two parts of 5 and
    6 hours make an 11-hour workday with 3 hours of daily overtime, even
    though neither part was longer than 8 hours.
  </p>

  <h2>California's split shift premium</h2>
  <p>
    California's wage orders define a split shift as "a work schedule which
    is interrupted by non-paid non-working periods established by the
    employer, other than bona fide rest or meal periods". When an employee
    works one, the wage orders require "one (1) hour's pay at the minimum wage"
    in addition to the minimum wage for that workday, unless the employee
    lives at the place of employment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The California Division of Labor Standards Enforcement applies this as a
    minimum for the day, not a flat bonus. Wages you earn above the minimum
    wage count toward the premium:
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>minimum for the day = (minimum wage &times; hours worked) + (1 &times; minimum wage)</code><br />
    <code>premium owed = minimum for the day − wages actually earned (if positive)</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the minimum wage that applies where you work. The 2026 statewide rate
    is $16.90 an hour, and many cities and counties set higher local rates;
    the DLSE uses whichever is higher.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 1: premium owed</h3>
  <p>
    An employee earning $17.00 an hour works 7:00 to 11:00 AM and 4:00 to
    8:00 PM, 8 hours in total, where the minimum wage is $16.90:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Minimum for the day: 8 &times; $16.90 + $16.90 = $135.20 + $16.90 = <strong>$152.10</strong></li>
    <li>Wages earned: 8 &times; $17.00 = <strong>$136.00</strong></li>
    <li>Premium owed: $152.10 − $136.00 = <strong>$16.10</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Another way to see it: the full premium is $16.90, and the employee
    already earns $0.10 an hour above minimum wage, or $0.80 over 8 hours, so
    $16.90 − $0.80 = $16.10 is still owed.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 2: no premium owed</h3>
  <p>
    The same split shift at $20.00 an hour earns $160.00, which is more than
    the $152.10 minimum for the day. The $3.10 an hour above minimum wage
    (&times; 8 = $24.80) more than covers the $16.90 premium, so nothing
    extra is owed.
  </p>

  <h3>When does the premium stop applying?</h3>
  <p>
    Because the premium is offset by pay above minimum wage, it only matters
    below a break-even hourly rate, which depends on how many hours you work
    that day. At the $16.90 statewide minimum:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hours worked on the split-shift day</th><th>Premium owed if paid less than</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>$21.13 an hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>$19.72 an hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>$19.02 an hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>$18.59 an hour</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The break-even rate is the minimum wage &times; (1 + 1 &divide; hours
    worked), rounded up to the cent. With a higher local minimum wage, the
    break-even rates are higher too.
  </p>

  <h2>What isn't a split shift</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A normal meal break.</strong> A bona fide unpaid meal period
      doesn't make a split shift, even if it's long-ish.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A shift that crosses into the next workday.</strong> In
      <em>Securitas Security Services USA, Inc. v. Superior Court</em> (2011),
      the California Court of Appeal held that a shift spanning two workdays
      isn't a split shift. See <a href="/guides/overnight-shift-hours/">overnight
      shift hours</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A gap you chose.</strong> The definition covers non-working
      periods "established by the employer".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two separate jobs</strong> with different employers on the same day.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outside California</h2>
  <p>
    US federal law has no split shift premium. You're paid for the hours you
    work, and the unpaid gap in the middle isn't work time as long as you're
    genuinely free to use it for yourself. If you're required to stay on the
    premises or on call, that time may count as hours worked. Elsewhere,
    split shift allowances usually come from union contracts or, in
    Australia, from the relevant modern award.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculate it</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/">Hours Worked
    Calculator</a> takes up to three sessions in a day and adds them up. For a
    full week of split shifts with overtime, use the
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a>; then
    check the California premium by hand with the formula above.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/california/8-CCR-11040" rel="noopener">California Code of Regulations, Title 8, §11040 — split shift definition and premium (§2, §4(C))</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/minimum_wage.htm" rel="noopener">California DIR — Minimum wage</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/ca-court-of-appeal/1573627.html" rel="noopener">Securitas Security Services USA, Inc. v. Superior Court (Cal. Ct. App. 2011)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/22-flsa-hours-worked" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #22: Hours worked (on-call and waiting time)</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
